# PROBE range-no-overderivation. Two complex fillers under one range must not be merged (the range-complex-fillers hazard): nothing is unsatisfiable, yet the range still reaches both successors (∃r.R ⊑ D gives C1 ⊑ D and C2 ⊑ D) — the positive half is what shows the range was processed at all.
# construct: ObjectPropertyRange
# expect: sub :C1 :D
# expect: sub :C2 :D
# expect-not: unsat :C1
# expect-not: unsat :C2

@prefix :     <http://example.org/t#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:  <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

<http://example.org/t> a owl:Ontology .
:C1 a owl:Class . :D a owl:Class . :C2 a owl:Class . :P a owl:Class . :Q a owl:Class . :U a owl:Class . :V a owl:Class . :R a owl:Class .
:r a owl:ObjectProperty .
:r rdfs:range :R .
:P owl:disjointWith :U .
:C1 rdfs:subClassOf [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty :r ; owl:someValuesFrom [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( :P :Q ) ] ] .
:C2 rdfs:subClassOf [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty :r ; owl:someValuesFrom [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( :U :V ) ] ] .
[ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty :r ; owl:someValuesFrom :R ] rdfs:subClassOf :D .
